Oracle Native Network Encryption (NNE) encrypts data in transit (in-flight) between the Oracle client and the Oracle database server.

✅ Encrypts SQL queries, results, usernames, passwords, and other network traffic.
✅ Works without requiring SSL/TLS certificates.
✅ Uses Oracle Net encryption configured in sqlnet.ora on the client and server.
✅ Supports encryption algorithms such as AES (AES128, AES192, AES256) depending on the Oracle version.


However:

NNE is not SSL/TLS. It is Oracle's own encryption mechanism built into Oracle Net.
SSL/TLS uses X.509 certificates to provide encryption and server/client identity verification.
NNE provides encryption (and optionally checksumming for integrity), but it does not provide the same certificate-based authentication model that TLS offers.


A simple comparison:

Oracle Native Network Encryption	SSL/TLS
Encrypts data in transit	Encrypts data in transit
No certificates required	Certificates required
Uses Oracle Net encryption	Uses standard TLS protocol
Easier to configure	More configuration (wallets/certificates)


So your statement can be refined to:

Oracle Native Network Encryption encrypts the in-flight data exchanged between the Oracle client and the database server without requiring SSL/TLS certificates. It uses Oracle's native network encryption instead of the TLS protocol.


